      "answer": "TL;DR: There\u2019s no direct evidence about Thalia\u2019s trustworthiness in the projection, but there are indirect red flags\u2014something could be off, and caution is warranted.\n\nBased on the available facts, Thalia herself is not described in the projection, so we lack explicit information about her behavior, motives, or condition in the emergency meeting.\n\nHowever, there are relevant indirect signals:\n- OBSERVED: Stafl \u201cAttacks Thalia after being revived,\u201d which suggests either Thalia was perceived as a threat or something about her triggered hostility.\n- OBSERVED: Caelynn is \u201cTalking with Thalia about the plans\u201d and trusts her enough to assign a task (\u201cInstructs Thalia to bring a potion to Lesandra\u201d), indicating at least some level of operational trust from the party.\n\nThese facts conflict in implication: one ally collaborates with Thalia, while another attacks her. There\u2019s no resolution provided, so the current state is ambiguous.\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Relationships: Trusted by Caelynn for tasks; attacked by Stafl under unclear circumstances\n- Role in Events: Involved in planning and potion delivery\n- Known Issues: None explicitly stated (no confirmed corruption, deception, or abnormal condition)\n\nConclusion: You don\u2019t have enough confirmed evidence to fully trust or distrust Thalia. Treat her as uncertain\u2014keep her involved, but verify her actions closely.",
