      "stale_hits": [],
      "global_stale_hits": [],
      "answer": "TL;DR: Thalia was **not truly corrupted**\u2014she was **ensorcelled and manipulated by the Wolf**, who used that influence to direct her actions.\n\nThalia\u2019s state is consistently described as **\u201censorcelled by the Wolf\u201d (OBSERVED)** and even **\u201censorcelled (unbeknownst to her)\u201d**, which aligns with her **\u201cInnocent\u201d mental state (OBSERVED)** and the fact that her **\u201cCharmed status ended.\u201d** Crucially, she **\u201cdoes not have an oily sheen to her eyes,\u201d** which is a known marker of corruption in others (e.g., guards and the Wolf).\n\nBy contrast, the Wolf is explicitly tied to corruption:\n- **\u201cWolf has an oily sheen to his eyes.\u201d (OBSERVED)**\n- Identified as a **cultist** and **corrupting influence**\n\nThe connection is direct:  \n- The Wolf **\u201corders Thalia to attack the group\u201d (OBSERVED)**  \n- Thalia **serves the Wolf** but can also be **convinced of his betrayal**, indicating her loyalty was compromised, not inherent.\n\nSo the current canon+observed synthesis is:\n- Thalia = **controlled/played (ensorcelled), not corrupted**\n- The Wolf = **active corrupter/manipulator**, using magic and deception to turn others into tools\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Thalia: ensorcelled, no oily eyes, innocent, Commander of the Guard, can be persuaded\n- The Wolf: cultist, oily eyes, deceptive, issues commands to Thalia, source of influence",
      "embedding_similarity": null
